# Build-context denylist for the apex (Level 1) JavaScript tier.
#
# The sibling Dockerfile copies its runtime files BY NAME, so this file does not
# decide what lands in the image — it decides what the daemon has to receive before
# the build starts. Kept an explicit denylist rather than a `*` with `!`
# re-inclusions, where one mis-ordered negation drops a file silently.
#
# Never exclude package.json or config/health.json: health.js falls back to
# compiled-in literals rather than failing, so their absence would leave the
# endpoint answering 200 with an identity this repository never declared — a failure
# with no symptom. So no broad *.json, config or config/* pattern. (`*.test.js` is
# safe: none of index.js, health.js or server.js ends in `.test.js`.)

# Version-control metadata. .git also carries local remote-tracking configuration
# that must not enter a layer.
.git
.gitignore
.gitmodules

# The child submodule's working tree: each tier builds its own image from its own
# sources. A build-context exclusion only — the submodule stays tracked in Git.
child_repo_10_LOC

# Build-time-only inputs, read from the context before the build.
.dockerignore
Dockerfile

# A .github directory, whenever one is added: read from the repository, never from
# inside an image.
.github

# Local environment files, including the tracked .env.example template. Nothing here
# parses one — this tier reads process.env directly.
.env*

# This tier installs nothing, so this keeps a stale host directory out of the
# context; it is not what protects the image, since the Dockerfile copies named
# files only.
node_modules

# Test sources. A runtime image ships no test.
*.test.js

# Documentation, including the normative contract document, which health.js and
# server.js reference in comments and never open.
docs
README.md

# Execution residue from a local run. Not reproducible input.
coverage
.npm
npm-debug.log*
*.log

# Operating-system metadata that file managers leave behind. Never content.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Deliberately NOT excluded: .nvmrc, which keeps /app consistent with the
# repository. Python and Java residue patterns are absent because this tier cannot
# produce them; Levels 2 and 3 exclude their own.
